{
  "hub": {
    "id": "concern-frontend-stack",
    "category": "concern",
    "name": "Frontend stack",
    "description": "The UI framework and rendering model of a web front end, its design system, the browsers it supports, its performance budget (Core Web Vitals, JavaScript size) and its accessibility level.",
    "tags": [
      "technology",
      "frontend",
      "web",
      "framework",
      "core web vitals",
      "accessibility",
      "design system",
      "browser support"
    ],
    "requiredMetamodel": "c4-ddd-governance-builtin",
    "concern": {
      "id": "frontend-stack",
      "trigger": "LIST NODES WHERE type = \"webapp\" AND external != true",
      "why": "these are web front ends, so what they are built with, how they render, how fast and accessible they must be, and which browsers they support has to be decided.",
      "group": "technology"
    },
    "sources": [
      {
        "title": "web.dev: Web Vitals (LCP, INP, CLS and their thresholds)",
        "url": "https://web.dev/articles/vitals"
      },
      {
        "title": "web.dev: Performance budgets 101",
        "url": "https://web.dev/articles/performance-budgets-101"
      },
      {
        "title": "web.dev: Rendering on the web",
        "url": "https://web.dev/articles/rendering-on-the-web"
      },
      {
        "title": "web.dev: Time to First Byte (TTFB)",
        "url": "https://web.dev/articles/ttfb"
      },
      {
        "title": "web.dev: Prevent unnecessary network requests with the HTTP Cache",
        "url": "https://web.dev/articles/http-cache"
      },
      {
        "title": "W3C: Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2",
        "url": "https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/"
      },
      {
        "title": "ETSI EN 301 549: Accessibility requirements for ICT products and services",
        "url": "https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_en/301500_301599/301549/03.02.01_60/en_301549v030201p.pdf"
      },
      {
        "title": "W3C Design Tokens Community Group: Design Tokens Format Module",
        "url": "https://tr.designtokens.org/format/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Tailwind CSS documentation: theme variables",
        "url": "https://tailwindcss.com/docs/theme"
      },
      {
        "title": "Browserslist",
        "url": "https://github.com/browserslist/browserslist"
      },
      {
        "title": "endoflife.date: framework support lifecycles",
        "url": "https://endoflife.date/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Lighthouse CI",
        "url": "https://github.com/GoogleChrome/lighthouse-ci"
      },
      {
        "title": "axe-core",
        "url": "https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core"
      },
      {
        "title": "Thoughtworks Technology Radar",
        "url": "https://www.thoughtworks.com/radar"
      }
    ],
    "templateParams": [
      {
        "key": "FRAMEWORK",
        "title": "Framework",
        "label": "What is the front end built with?",
        "hint": "The framework brings its ecosystem, its way of rendering and the people who can work on it. Changing it later means rewriting the front end.",
        "defaultValue": "react",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "react",
            "label": "React (Next.js or Vite)",
            "description": "The largest ecosystem and hiring pool; Next.js for server rendering and static pages, Vite for a plain single-page app. Pick it when the team knows React, or you need the widest choice of components and libraries."
          },
          {
            "value": "angular",
            "label": "Angular",
            "description": "One opinionated framework with routing, forms, HTTP and testing included, and a published support schedule per major. Pick it for large business applications built by several teams that value one way of doing things, or where the team already knows it."
          },
          {
            "value": "vue",
            "label": "Vue (Nuxt or Vite)",
            "description": "Approachable, with single-file components; Nuxt for server rendering and static pages. Pick it when the team knows Vue, or it is adopted gradually into existing server-rendered pages."
          },
          {
            "value": "svelte",
            "label": "Svelte (SvelteKit)",
            "description": "Compiles to small JavaScript, with SvelteKit for routing and every rendering mode. Pick it when bundle size and simplicity weigh most and a smaller ecosystem is acceptable."
          },
          {
            "value": "htmx",
            "label": "server-rendered HTML with htmx",
            "description": "The back end renders HTML from templates; htmx swaps parts of the page, and Alpine.js holds small client state. Pick it for forms-and-tables applications when the team is back-end heavy and rich client-side interaction is rare. The rendering question does not apply: every page is rendered on the server (answer SSR)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "key": "RENDERING",
        "title": "Rendering",
        "label": "Where are pages rendered?",
        "hint": "Rendering decides what the first response holds: an empty shell, the content, or a file built in advance. It sets first-load speed, search visibility and what the server must do. Only when the framework is not htmx, which renders every page on the server: answer SSR there.",
        "defaultValue": "ssr",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "spa",
            "label": "in the browser (single-page app)",
            "description": "The server sends a shell and the browser renders everything. Pick it for applications behind sign-in, used for long sessions, where search engines do not matter and later navigation must be instant. Not with htmx."
          },
          {
            "value": "ssr",
            "label": "on the server for each request (SSR)",
            "description": "Each request gets HTML with its content, then the page becomes interactive. Pick it for public pages with personalised or fast-changing content where search and first load matter, and always with htmx."
          },
          {
            "value": "static",
            "label": "at build time (static generation)",
            "description": "Pages are built in advance and served from a CDN, rebuilt when content changes. Pick it for content that is the same for everyone and changes at most a few times an hour (marketing, documentation, catalogues). Not with htmx."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "key": "DESIGN",
        "title": "Design system",
        "label": "What are the screens built from?",
        "hint": "A design system keeps screens consistent and accessible; the question is whether you build it, adopt one, or compose one from utilities.",
        "defaultValue": "library",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "own",
            "label": "our own design system",
            "description": "Our components and design tokens, built on accessible headless primitives (Radix or React Aria for React, Angular CDK, Reka UI for Vue, Bits UI for Svelte; template partials with htmx), documented in Storybook. Pick it when the brand must be distinct and several applications share it, and a team can own it."
          },
          {
            "value": "library",
            "label": "an established component library",
            "description": "An off-the-shelf library themed to the brand: MUI, Angular Material, Vuetify, PrimeNG, Fluent UI, Skeleton; with htmx a CSS library such as Bootstrap or Pico. Pick it for business applications where speed matters more than a distinct look."
          },
          {
            "value": "utility",
            "label": "utility CSS with copied components",
            "description": "Tailwind CSS with the design tokens in its theme, and components copied into the codebase (shadcn/ui and similar). Pick it when you want full control of the markup without maintaining a separate library."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "key": "BROWSERS",
        "title": "Browser support",
        "label": "Which browsers must work?",
        "hint": "The browser list decides which language features and CSS ship untranspiled, which polyfills load, and which browsers the tests run in.",
        "defaultValue": "defaults",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "modern",
            "label": "the last two versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari",
            "description": "Only current evergreen browsers; older ones get a notice naming the supported browsers. Pick it for internal tools and applications whose users have managed, up-to-date devices."
          },
          {
            "value": "defaults",
            "label": "browserslist defaults (over 0.5% use, last 2 versions, Firefox ESR)",
            "description": "The broad default most public sites use. Pick it for public sites and applications with a general audience."
          },
          {
            "value": "extended",
            "label": "an extended list, including older phones (iOS 15 and later)",
            "description": "Over 0.2% use, last 3 versions, Firefox ESR, iOS Safari 15 and later, with release candidates tried on real old phones. Pick it when analytics show many users on older phones (emerging markets, public services that must reach everyone)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "key": "LCP_MS",
        "label": "Largest Contentful Paint at the 75th percentile (ms)?",
        "defaultValue": "2500",
        "type": "number"
      },
      {
        "key": "INP_MS",
        "label": "Interaction to Next Paint at the 75th percentile (ms)?",
        "defaultValue": "200",
        "type": "number"
      },
      {
        "key": "CLS",
        "label": "Cumulative Layout Shift at the 75th percentile?",
        "defaultValue": "0.1",
        "type": "number"
      },
      {
        "key": "JS_KB",
        "label": "At most how much JavaScript may a route load before it is interactive (KB, compressed)?",
        "defaultValue": "200",
        "type": "number"
      },
      {
        "key": "EOL_DAYS",
        "label": "How many days before end of support must a framework major be replaced?",
        "defaultValue": "180",
        "type": "number"
      }
    ]
  },
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "need",
      "type": "need",
      "label": "A fast, accessible front end",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "kind": "brief",
      "source": "Hub concern pack: Frontend stack",
      "description": "People use this front end on their own devices, browsers and networks, some with disabilities and assistive technology. It has to load and respond quickly on an ordinary phone, work in the browsers its users have, be usable by everyone, look and behave consistently, and be built on a framework the team knows and that keeps receiving fixes. The choices and the numbers it is held to must be written down and checked, not rediscovered after users complain. The checks assume one front end per repository with its package.json at the root, a versions.txt listing the endoflife.date products and cycles in use, server templates (htmx) under templates/ or src/, and a RUM table rum_events(name, value, page_group, received_at) fed by the page."
    },
    {
      "id": "req-one-framework",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "Built on the chosen framework",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 0,
      "ears_type": "ubiquitous",
      "action": "build the front end with {{FRAMEWORK}}, adding another UI framework only after a new decision record supersedes this one",
      "rationale": "Two UI frameworks in one front end double the JavaScript users download and the skills the team needs (Thoughtworks Technology Radar)."
    },
    {
      "id": "req-vitals",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "Core Web Vitals met",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 130,
      "ears_type": "ubiquitous",
      "action": "meet, at the 75th percentile of real page loads over 28 days per page group, a Largest Contentful Paint within {{LCP_MS}} ms, an Interaction to Next Paint within {{INP_MS}} ms and a Cumulative Layout Shift of at most {{CLS}}",
      "rationale": "These are the thresholds for a \"good\" experience, measured at the 75th percentile of field data over 28 days (web.dev, Web Vitals: LCP 2.5 s, INP 200 ms, CLS 0.1); they also feed search ranking."
    },
    {
      "id": "req-bundle",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "JavaScript within budget",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 260,
      "ears_type": "ubiquitous",
      "action": "keep the JavaScript a route loads before it is interactive within {{JS_KB}} KB compressed, loading the rest on demand",
      "rationale": "JavaScript is the costliest byte on a phone: it is downloaded, parsed and run on the main thread, which is what INP measures (web.dev, Performance budgets 101)."
    },
    {
      "id": "req-a11y",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "Accessible to WCAG 2.2 AA",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 390,
      "ears_type": "ubiquitous",
      "action": "conform to WCAG 2.2 level AA on every page, with 0 serious or critical automated accessibility violations, every function operable by keyboard alone, and a manual audit with a screen reader before each major release",
      "rationale": "WCAG 2.2 AA is the level EN 301 549, the European Accessibility Act and most public-sector rules require; automated checks find only part of the failures, hence the keyboard journey and the manual audit (W3C WCAG 2.2; EN 301 549)."
    },
    {
      "id": "req-supported",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "Supported framework versions",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 520,
      "ears_type": "ubiquitous",
      "action": "run only framework and build-tool majors under support, moving off each at least {{EOL_DAYS}} days before its end of support",
      "rationale": "Front-end frameworks support a major for a limited time (Angular 18 months, Node.js-based tooling with its runtime); after that, security fixes stop (endoflife.date).",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "react",
          "angular",
          "vue",
          "svelte"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "req-spa-cache",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "App shell cached right",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 650,
      "ears_type": "event-driven",
      "trigger": "a new version of the single-page app is deployed",
      "action": "serve its content-hashed assets with a one-year immutable cache and its HTML with no-cache, so that every user gets the new version on their next navigation and 0 users load a mix of versions",
      "rationale": "A single-page app is a shell plus assets; wrong caching either serves stale code for days or downloads everything on every visit (web.dev, HTTP Cache).",
      "hubWhen": {
        "RENDERING": [
          "spa"
        ],
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "react",
          "angular",
          "vue",
          "svelte"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "req-ssr",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "Content in the first response",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 780,
      "ears_type": "event-driven",
      "trigger": "a page is requested",
      "action": "answer with HTML that already holds the page's main content, with a Time to First Byte within 800 ms at the 75th percentile at the peak load decided in the Capacity and scaling pack",
      "rationale": "Server rendering pays off only if the server answers quickly; 800 ms is web.dev's \"good\" Time to First Byte, which leaves room for LCP within budget (web.dev, TTFB).",
      "hubWhen": {
        "RENDERING": [
          "ssr"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "req-static",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "Pages built in advance",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 910,
      "ears_type": "event-driven",
      "trigger": "content is published or changed",
      "action": "rebuild the affected pages and serve them from the CDN within 15 minutes, with 100% of pages served as pre-built files from the CDN cache",
      "rationale": "Static pages are the fastest and cheapest to serve; the publishing delay is the price, and it must be bounded (web.dev, Rendering on the web).",
      "hubWhen": {
        "RENDERING": [
          "static"
        ],
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "react",
          "angular",
          "vue",
          "svelte"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "req-design-own",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "Screens use our design system",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 1040,
      "ears_type": "ubiquitous",
      "action": "build every screen from the design system's components and tokens, document each component with its states in Storybook, and use 0 hard-coded colours, font sizes or spacing outside the tokens",
      "rationale": "A design system pays off only when used everywhere; tokens make a brand or contrast change one edit instead of a search (W3C Design Tokens Format Module).",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DESIGN": [
          "own"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "req-design-library",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "Library used through its theme",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 1170,
      "ears_type": "ubiquitous",
      "action": "style the component library only through its theme and public props, with 0 overrides of its internal class names, so that it can be upgraded to each new major within 6 months",
      "rationale": "Overrides of a library's internals break on its next major and pin the front end to an old version (the library's own theming guides).",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DESIGN": [
          "library"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "req-design-utility",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "Utilities use the tokens",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 1300,
      "ears_type": "ubiquitous",
      "action": "take colours, spacing and type sizes only from the tokens in the Tailwind theme, with 0 arbitrary values in class names",
      "rationale": "Arbitrary values bypass the theme and turn the design system back into one-off styles (Tailwind CSS documentation, theme variables).",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DESIGN": [
          "utility"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "req-browsers",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "Supported browsers work",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 1430,
      "ears_type": "ubiquitous",
      "action": "work in every browser of {{BROWSERS}}, with the main journeys tested in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit on every pull request and 0 unsupported language or web features shipped without a polyfill",
      "rationale": "An explicit browser list lets the build ship only the transpilation and polyfills needed, and the lint catch a feature the list does not have (Browserslist)."
    },
    {
      "id": "req-unsupported",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "Old browsers told",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 1560,
      "ears_type": "unwanted-behaviour",
      "unwanted_condition": "the front end is opened in a browser outside the supported list",
      "action": "show a notice naming the supported browsers instead of a broken page",
      "rationale": "A short browser list is acceptable only if users outside it learn why the page fails (Browserslist).",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BROWSERS": [
          "modern"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "req-old-devices",
      "type": "requirement",
      "label": "Tested on older phones",
      "x": 260,
      "y": 1690,
      "ears_type": "event-driven",
      "trigger": "a release candidate is built",
      "action": "run the main journeys on real devices with the oldest supported iOS Safari and a low-end Android phone, and meet the Core Web Vitals budget on the Android phone",
      "rationale": "Emulated browsers miss the CPU and memory limits of old phones, where an extended audience feels the budget most (web.dev, Web Vitals).",
      "unchecked": "It runs on real phones, which no pipeline tool here drives: the release engineer runs the main journeys on the device bench (the oldest supported iPhone and a low-end Android phone) before each release candidate is promoted, and records LCP from the phone's DevTools in the release notes.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BROWSERS": [
          "extended"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-one-framework",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "Second framework refused",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 0,
      "given": "the front end's package.json files",
      "when": "a change adds another UI framework's package",
      "then": "the framework check fails until a decision record allows it"
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-vitals",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "Field vitals within budget",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 130,
      "given": "28 days of real-user measurements per page group",
      "when": "the 75th percentiles are computed",
      "then": "LCP is within {{LCP_MS}} ms, INP within {{INP_MS}} ms and CLS at most {{CLS}} for every page group"
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-bundle",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "Bundle over budget refused",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 260,
      "given": "a pull request that adds a large library to a route",
      "when": "Lighthouse CI runs against the preview",
      "then": "it fails when the route's script transfer size passes {{JS_KB}} KB"
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-a11y",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "Page passes accessibility checks",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 390,
      "given": "each page of the main journeys",
      "when": "the automated checks run and the main journey is completed with the keyboard only",
      "then": "0 serious or critical violations are reported and the journey completes"
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-supported",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "End of support ahead",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 520,
      "given": "a framework major whose end of support is less than {{EOL_DAYS}} days away",
      "when": "the version check runs",
      "then": "it fails naming the version and its end-of-support date",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "react",
          "angular",
          "vue",
          "svelte"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-spa-cache",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "New version reaches users",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 650,
      "given": "a user with the previous version cached",
      "when": "a new version is deployed and the user navigates",
      "then": "the new HTML loads, its hashed assets are fetched once, and the unchanged ones come from the cache",
      "hubWhen": {
        "RENDERING": [
          "spa"
        ],
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "react",
          "angular",
          "vue",
          "svelte"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-ssr",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "Content without JavaScript",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 780,
      "given": "a public page",
      "when": "it is requested at the peak rate with JavaScript disabled",
      "then": "its main content is in the HTML, and the 75th-percentile TTFB is within 800 ms",
      "hubWhen": {
        "RENDERING": [
          "ssr"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-static",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "Change live within 15 minutes",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 910,
      "given": "a published page",
      "when": "its content is changed",
      "then": "the publish run completes within 15 minutes and the CDN serves the rebuilt page from its cache",
      "hubWhen": {
        "RENDERING": [
          "static"
        ],
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "react",
          "angular",
          "vue",
          "svelte"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-design-own",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "Hard-coded colour refused",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 1040,
      "given": "a stylesheet change",
      "when": "it sets a colour as a hex value instead of a token",
      "then": "the style lint fails",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DESIGN": [
          "own"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-design-library",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "Internal override refused",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 1170,
      "given": "a stylesheet change",
      "when": "it targets one of the library's internal class names",
      "then": "the style lint fails",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DESIGN": [
          "library"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-design-utility",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "Arbitrary value refused",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 1300,
      "given": "a component or template change",
      "when": "it uses a class with an arbitrary value such as a hex colour in brackets",
      "then": "the lint fails",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DESIGN": [
          "utility"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-browsers",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "Unsupported feature caught",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 1430,
      "given": "the browser list",
      "when": "a change uses a web feature a listed browser lacks",
      "then": "the compatibility lint fails unless a polyfill is configured"
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-unsupported",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "Notice in old browser",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 1560,
      "given": "a browser lacking a feature every supported browser has",
      "when": "the front end is opened",
      "then": "the notice names the supported browsers",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BROWSERS": [
          "modern"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sc-old-devices",
      "type": "scenario",
      "label": "Journeys pass on old phones",
      "x": 520,
      "y": 1690,
      "given": "a release candidate",
      "when": "the main journeys run on the oldest supported iPhone and a low-end Android phone",
      "then": "all pass, and LCP on the Android phone is within {{LCP_MS}} ms",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BROWSERS": [
          "extended"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-react",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Built with React",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 0,
      "concern": "frontend-stack",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "This element is a web front end that people use in their own browsers, on their own devices and networks. The forces: every kilobyte of JavaScript is downloaded, parsed and run on the user's phone, and mid-range phones on mobile networks are where most budgets break; search engines and first visits reward content in the first HTML response, while app-like screens favour rendering in the browser; a framework brings its ecosystem, hiring pool and release cadence, and its majors reach end of support on published dates; a shared design system keeps screens consistent and accessible, but costs effort to build and keep; the browsers to support decide which features and polyfills ship; and accessibility is a legal duty in many markets (European Accessibility Act, ADA, Section 508), cheap when built in and expensive when retrofitted.",
      "decision": "The front end is built with React in TypeScript (strict). Rendering: {{RENDERING}}: Next.js (App Router, server components) for server rendering and static generation, Vite with React Router for a single-page app. Data fetching with TanStack Query; forms with React Hook Form. Design system: {{DESIGN}}. Browsers: {{BROWSERS}}. Budget: LCP {{LCP_MS}} ms, INP {{INP_MS}} ms, CLS {{CLS}} at the 75th percentile of real page loads, {{JS_KB}} KB of JavaScript per route; WCAG 2.2 AA. Dependency updates: as decided in the Dependency hygiene pack. Choosing Next.js or React Router (framework mode): Next.js for the widest ecosystem and hosting on Vercel or Node; React Router when you want web-standard loaders and fewer conventions. Versions tracked through endoflife.date (react, nextjs), each major replaced at least {{EOL_DAYS}} days before its end of support.",
      "consequences": "• The largest ecosystem of components and libraries, and the largest hiring pool.\n• React itself is only the view: routing, data, forms and state are choices to make and keep current.\n• Server components cut the JavaScript sent, but add a server and a new mental model.\n• Next.js majors arrive roughly yearly and change conventions; upgrades are planned work.",
      "alternatives": "1. Angular: everything included and one way of doing it, heavier.\n2. Vue with Nuxt: similar capabilities, gentler learning curve, smaller ecosystem.\n3. Svelte with SvelteKit: smaller bundles, smaller ecosystem.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "react"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-angular",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Built with Angular",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 130,
      "concern": "frontend-stack",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "This element is a web front end that people use in their own browsers, on their own devices and networks. The forces: every kilobyte of JavaScript is downloaded, parsed and run on the user's phone, and mid-range phones on mobile networks are where most budgets break; search engines and first visits reward content in the first HTML response, while app-like screens favour rendering in the browser; a framework brings its ecosystem, hiring pool and release cadence, and its majors reach end of support on published dates; a shared design system keeps screens consistent and accessible, but costs effort to build and keep; the browsers to support decide which features and polyfills ship; and accessibility is a legal duty in many markets (European Accessibility Act, ADA, Section 508), cheap when built in and expensive when retrofitted.",
      "decision": "The front end is built with Angular in TypeScript (strict), with standalone components, signals and the Angular CLI (esbuild). Rendering: {{RENDERING}}: Angular SSR with hydration for server rendering, prerendering for static pages, the default build for a single-page app. Design system: {{DESIGN}} (Angular Material and the CDK when adopting a library). Browsers: {{BROWSERS}}. Budget: LCP {{LCP_MS}} ms, INP {{INP_MS}} ms, CLS {{CLS}} at the 75th percentile of real page loads, {{JS_KB}} KB of JavaScript per route; WCAG 2.2 AA. Dependency updates: as decided in the Dependency hygiene pack. The JavaScript budget is also set in angular.json budgets. Each major is supported for 18 months (6 active, 12 LTS), so the front end moves one major at a time with ng update, at least {{EOL_DAYS}} days before the end of support tracked on endoflife.date (angular).",
      "consequences": "• Routing, forms, HTTP, testing and i18n come with the framework: one way of doing things across teams.\n• Larger initial bundles than Svelte or Vue unless routes are lazy-loaded and deferrable views used.\n• A predictable release and support schedule makes the version policy easy to plan.\n• A steeper learning curve (dependency injection, RxJS) for people new to it.",
      "alternatives": "1. React with Next.js: larger ecosystem, more choices to make.\n2. Vue with Nuxt: lighter, less prescriptive.\n3. Server-rendered HTML with htmx: far less JavaScript for forms-and-tables applications.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "angular"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-vue",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Built with Vue",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 260,
      "concern": "frontend-stack",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "This element is a web front end that people use in their own browsers, on their own devices and networks. The forces: every kilobyte of JavaScript is downloaded, parsed and run on the user's phone, and mid-range phones on mobile networks are where most budgets break; search engines and first visits reward content in the first HTML response, while app-like screens favour rendering in the browser; a framework brings its ecosystem, hiring pool and release cadence, and its majors reach end of support on published dates; a shared design system keeps screens consistent and accessible, but costs effort to build and keep; the browsers to support decide which features and polyfills ship; and accessibility is a legal duty in many markets (European Accessibility Act, ADA, Section 508), cheap when built in and expensive when retrofitted.",
      "decision": "The front end is built with Vue 3 in TypeScript, using the Composition API and single-file components. Rendering: {{RENDERING}}: Nuxt for server rendering and static generation, Vite with Vue Router for a single-page app. State in Pinia. Design system: {{DESIGN}}. Browsers: {{BROWSERS}}. Budget: LCP {{LCP_MS}} ms, INP {{INP_MS}} ms, CLS {{CLS}} at the 75th percentile of real page loads, {{JS_KB}} KB of JavaScript per route; WCAG 2.2 AA. Dependency updates: as decided in the Dependency hygiene pack. Versions tracked through endoflife.date (vue, nuxt), each major replaced at least {{EOL_DAYS}} days before its end of support.",
      "consequences": "• Gentle learning curve and good performance out of the box.\n• Can be adopted gradually inside existing server-rendered pages.\n• A smaller ecosystem and hiring pool than React.\n• Nuxt majors bring migration work; plan them like framework upgrades.",
      "alternatives": "1. React with Next.js: the largest ecosystem.\n2. Svelte with SvelteKit: smaller bundles.\n3. Angular: more included, more prescriptive.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "vue"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-svelte",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Built with Svelte",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 390,
      "concern": "frontend-stack",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "This element is a web front end that people use in their own browsers, on their own devices and networks. The forces: every kilobyte of JavaScript is downloaded, parsed and run on the user's phone, and mid-range phones on mobile networks are where most budgets break; search engines and first visits reward content in the first HTML response, while app-like screens favour rendering in the browser; a framework brings its ecosystem, hiring pool and release cadence, and its majors reach end of support on published dates; a shared design system keeps screens consistent and accessible, but costs effort to build and keep; the browsers to support decide which features and polyfills ship; and accessibility is a legal duty in many markets (European Accessibility Act, ADA, Section 508), cheap when built in and expensive when retrofitted.",
      "decision": "The front end is built with Svelte 5 (runes) in TypeScript and SvelteKit, whose adapters cover every rendering mode. Rendering: {{RENDERING}}: adapter-node or a platform adapter for server rendering, adapter-static for static pages and single-page apps. Design system: {{DESIGN}}. Browsers: {{BROWSERS}}. Budget: LCP {{LCP_MS}} ms, INP {{INP_MS}} ms, CLS {{CLS}} at the 75th percentile of real page loads, {{JS_KB}} KB of JavaScript per route; WCAG 2.2 AA. Dependency updates: as decided in the Dependency hygiene pack. Versions tracked through endoflife.date (svelte), each major replaced at least {{EOL_DAYS}} days before its end of support.",
      "consequences": "• Compiled components ship little JavaScript, which makes the budget easy to keep.\n• Simple syntax, quick to learn.\n• A smaller ecosystem: fewer ready-made components and integrations, more built in-house.\n• A smaller hiring pool than React, Angular or Vue.",
      "alternatives": "1. React with Next.js: the largest ecosystem, more JavaScript.\n2. Vue with Nuxt: similar simplicity, larger ecosystem.\n3. Server-rendered HTML with htmx: even less JavaScript, less interactivity.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "svelte"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-htmx",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Server-rendered HTML with htmx",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 520,
      "concern": "frontend-stack",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "This element is a web front end that people use in their own browsers, on their own devices and networks. The forces: every kilobyte of JavaScript is downloaded, parsed and run on the user's phone, and mid-range phones on mobile networks are where most budgets break; search engines and first visits reward content in the first HTML response, while app-like screens favour rendering in the browser; a framework brings its ecosystem, hiring pool and release cadence, and its majors reach end of support on published dates; a shared design system keeps screens consistent and accessible, but costs effort to build and keep; the browsers to support decide which features and polyfills ship; and accessibility is a legal duty in many markets (European Accessibility Act, ADA, Section 508), cheap when built in and expensive when retrofitted.",
      "decision": "The front end is HTML rendered by the back end from templates (Thymeleaf, Razor, Jinja, templ or the back end's own), with htmx swapping parts of the page and Alpine.js for small client-side state; no client-side framework or build step beyond CSS. Rendering: every page on the server, with partial updates over HTTP; the rendering question does not apply. Design system: {{DESIGN}}, delivered as template partials. Browsers: {{BROWSERS}}. Budget: LCP {{LCP_MS}} ms, INP {{INP_MS}} ms, CLS {{CLS}} at the 75th percentile of real page loads, {{JS_KB}} KB of JavaScript per route; WCAG 2.2 AA. Dependency updates: as decided in the Dependency hygiene pack. htmx and Alpine.js are pinned dependencies updated like any other; the runtime and framework that render the pages, and their support lifecycle, are decided in the Backend stack pack. Choose it while interactions are forms, tables, filters and dialogs; supersede it when screens need rich client state (offline, drag-and-drop editors, real-time collaboration).",
      "consequences": "• Very little JavaScript, no client-side state to keep in sync, one codebase and one team for front and back end.\n• Fast first loads; every interaction is a request, so latency to the server sets how responsive it feels.\n• Rich client-side interaction is awkward and grows into hand-written JavaScript.\n• Fewer ready-made components; the design system is built as template partials.",
      "alternatives": "1. A single-page app framework (React, Angular, Vue, Svelte): rich interaction, much more JavaScript and a second codebase.\n2. Hotwire (Turbo and Stimulus) or Phoenix LiveView: the same idea tied to Rails or Elixir.\n3. Plain server-rendered pages without htmx: simplest, full reloads.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "htmx"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-one-framework-react",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Only React",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 0,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 package.json files in the repository declare @angular/core, vue, nuxt, svelte, @sveltejs/kit",
      "description": "A job in every build reads the dependencies and devDependencies of every package.json in the repository and fails when one names another UI framework's package; a second framework then arrives only with the decision record that allows it and a change to this check. Runs on Linux (git, jq, GNU xargs and grep).\n\nTool: git with jq and grep (CI job)\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# fails when a package.json anywhere in the repository declares another UI framework\nset -euo pipefail\nother=$(git ls-files -- '*package.json' | xargs -r jq -r '((.dependencies // {}) + (.devDependencies // {})) | keys[]' \\\n  | grep -xE '@angular\\/core|vue|nuxt|svelte|@sveltejs\\/kit' || true)\n[ -z \"$other\" ] || { echo \"::error::another UI framework is declared: $(tr '\\n' ' ' <<<\"$other\")(ADR Built with React)\"; exit 1; }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "react"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-one-framework-angular",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Only Angular",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 130,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 package.json files in the repository declare react, react-dom, next, vue, nuxt, svelte, @sveltejs/kit",
      "description": "A job in every build reads the dependencies and devDependencies of every package.json in the repository and fails when one names another UI framework's package; a second framework then arrives only with the decision record that allows it and a change to this check. Runs on Linux (git, jq, GNU xargs and grep).\n\nTool: git with jq and grep (CI job)\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# fails when a package.json anywhere in the repository declares another UI framework\nset -euo pipefail\nother=$(git ls-files -- '*package.json' | xargs -r jq -r '((.dependencies // {}) + (.devDependencies // {})) | keys[]' \\\n  | grep -xE 'react|react-dom|next|vue|nuxt|svelte|@sveltejs\\/kit' || true)\n[ -z \"$other\" ] || { echo \"::error::another UI framework is declared: $(tr '\\n' ' ' <<<\"$other\")(ADR Built with Angular)\"; exit 1; }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "FRAMEWORK": [
          "angular"
        ]
      }
    },
